  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nPeriods of Rain Monday-Thursday...Quiet conditions are expected for \nthe remainder of today and tonight with clouds increasing late \ntonight ahead of the next system. This system is expected to bring \nperiods of light to moderate rain, gusty winds, and a few rumbles of \nthunder between late Monday afternoon/evening through Thursday. \nSoutherly flow will usher in above normal PWATs around 1.5-1.6 \ninches from Monday evening through Tuesday evening, when the biggest \nsurge of moisture is expected. PWATs and rain intensity decrease \nTuesday night through Thursday, as low-level winds gradually turn \nwesterly, cutting off Gulf moisture. A few rumbles of thunder from \nelevated storms are possible at times, mainly on Tuesday, given a \ncouple hundred J/kg of MUCAPE, but strong/severe storms are not \nexpected.\n\nGiven the prolonged with periods of rain, a washout is not expected. \nBut, the drought laden areas across the north could see some relief \nas probabilities for 1 inch or more of rain are 65-95% for locations \nnorth and west of the Fox Valley, with 45-65% in the Fox Valley east \nto Lake Michigan from Monday afternoon through Wednesday afternoon. \nThe probability for 2 inches or more decreased slightly from the \nprevious forecast with 25-55% for locations north and west of the \nFox Valley. However, additional light rain is expected after \nWednesday afternoon through Thursday as well.\n\nTemperatures...Above normal temperatures are expected through \nThursday, falling to normal on Friday through the weekend (highs in \nthe upper 40s to low 50s) following the prolonged rainy system. \nTuesday will be the warmest day with highs in the mid to upper 60s, \nbut the cloud cover and rain may cause slightly lower temperatures. \nA few sites may be near/set new record high minimum temperatures on \nTuesday, November 3.",
